1. == alone: two equal points are two keys
   Pt.new(1, 2) == Pt.new(1, 2)               true
   Pt.new(1, 2).eql?(Pt.new(1, 2))            false
   Pt.new(1, 2).hash == Pt.new(1, 2).hash     false
   { Pt(1,2) => :a, Pt(1,2) => :b }.size      2
   h[Pt.new(1, 2)]                            nil
2. what == alone still does, and what it does not
   [Pt(1,2)].include?(Pt(1,2))  (uses ==)     true
   [Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].uniq.size               2
   ([Pt(1,2)] - [Pt(1,2)]).size               1
   Set[Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].size                 2
   [Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].tally.size              2
   [Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].group_by(&:itself).size 2
3. eql? and hash together fix it
   Pt.new(1, 2).eql?(Pt.new(1, 2))            true
   Pt.new(1, 2).hash == Pt.new(1, 2).hash     true
   { Pt(1,2) => :a, Pt(1,2) => :b }.size      1
   h[Pt.new(1, 2)]                            :b
   [Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].uniq.size               1
   Set[Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].size                 1
   [Pt(1,2), Pt(1,2)].tally                   {Pt(1,2) => 2}
4. Struct and Data get both for free
   S.new(1, 2).eql?(S.new(1, 2))              true
   { S(1,2) => 1, S(1,2) => 2 }.size          1
   { D(1,2) => 1, D(1,2) => 2 }.size          1
   S.new(1, 2) == S.new(1, 2.0)               true
   S.new(1, 2).eql?(S.new(1, 2.0))            false
5. an Array key works by value; a mutated key is lost until rehash
   { [1, 2] => :arr }[[1, 2]]                 :arr
   key = [1]; h = { key => :arr }; key << 2   (the key changed after insertion)
   h[[1, 2]]                                  nil
   h[key]                                     nil
   h.keys                                     [[1, 2]]
   h.rehash; h[[1, 2]]                        :arr
6. 1 and 1.0 are == but not eql?
   1 == 1.0                                   true
   1.eql?(1.0)                                false
   1.hash == 1.0.hash                         false
   { 1 => :int }[1.0]                         nil
   { 1 => :int, 1.0 => :float }               {1 => :int, 1.0 => :float}
   [1, 1.0].uniq.size                         2
   Set[1, 1.0].size                           2
7. a String key is copied and frozen
   s = +"key"; h = { s => 1 }                 (a mutable String as the key)
   h.keys.first.frozen?                       true
   s.frozen?                                  false
   s.equal?(h.keys.first)                     false
   s << "!"; h["key"]                         1
   h["key!"]                                  nil
8. compare_by_identity switches to equal?
   two "a" literals as keys, by identity      2
   the same String object twice               3
9. the rules
   hash returning a String                    TypeError: no implicit conversion of String into Integer
   eql? without hash: keys kept               2
   hash without eql?: keys kept               2
   Object.instance_method(:eql?).owner        Kernel
   Object.instance_method(:hash).owner        Kernel
   Object.instance_method(:==).owner          BasicObject
